# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Phase 11 — CMS SPA at /admin/* on the main blog domain
#
# Next.js `output: 'export'` writes /admin as admin.html, not
# admin/index.html. The copy-admin-spa.mjs postbuild step materialises
# `out/admin/index.html` from `admin/out/admin.html`, and nested routes
# land as `out/admin/<route>/index.html` — those resolve via Pages's
# directory-index lookup (verified: /admin/login/, /admin/blog/ work).
#
# The bare `/admin` and `/admin/` paths are the exception — Pages doesn't
# pick up out/admin/index.html for them, returning 404. Explicit 200
# rewrites fix that without changing the browser URL.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/admin                   /admin/index.html        200
/admin/                  /admin/index.html        200

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tag redirects — legacy double-encoded tag URLs
#
# The tag pages used to be written to disk with the tag name already
# percent-encoded (`out/ja/blog/tag/%E4%BD%9C%E5%86%99.html`), while the
# CDN looks a request up by the path it decoded. Every tag with a space
# or a non-ASCII character therefore 404'd; only a plain ASCII tag like
# `writing` survived, because encodeURIComponent leaves it untouched.
#
# The pages are now written with the raw tag name, so the URL form has
# NOT changed: /<locale>/<collection>/tag/<encodeURIComponent(tag)> is
# still canonical and now returns 200.
#
# What changed is the file name. The old file name was only reachable
# through a double-encoded URL, so those legacy URLs get a permanent
# redirect to the canonical one. A single-encoded URL is deliberately
# NOT redirected — it is the canonical form and already returns 200.
#
# Rule shape: <old double-encoded path>  <canonical path>  301
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
